<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drogheda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Drogheda is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-boyne">River Boyne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-louth">County Louth</a>, eastern Ireland. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a>, it was one of the principal walled towns of the English colony and sometimes hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a>, including the one that passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poynings-law">Poynings' Law</a> in 1494. In September 1649 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> stormed it and massacred much of the royalist garrison and many civilians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drogheda.jpg" alt="Drogheda" width="320" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stephen Durnin, William Murphy · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DroghedaLouthMeath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
